@startuml baseline-computation
title US-04: Reference Route Model - Static Waypoints Seeded at Startup (not computed from history)

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Load\nGenerator" as LoadGen
database "TimescaleDB\nroute_references" as RouteRef
database "TimescaleDB\nroute_reference_points" as RoutePoints
participant "Deviation\nDetector" as Detector

note over LoadGen
  At startup, the synthetic load generator
  seeds known route definitions into TimescaleDB.
  Real ADS-B/AIS entities have no known route —
  they are skipped by the Deviation Detector.
end note

LoadGen -> RouteRef : INSERT INTO route_references\n{ route_id, entity_id, description }

LoadGen -> RoutePoints : INSERT INTO route_reference_points\n{ route_id, sequence_index, lat, lon,\n  tolerance_metres }\n(one row per waypoint)

note over RoutePoints
  Waypoints define the expected corridor.
  tolerance_metres = per-waypoint deviation threshold.
  Seeded from the generator's known route definition —
  deterministic, injectable, and consistent with
  the anomalies the generator will produce.
  No statistical averaging of lat/lon positions.
  (Averaging A→B→C produces a midpoint, not a route.)
end note

Detector -> RouteRef : SELECT entity_id FROM route_references\nWHERE entity_id = $entity_id
RouteRef --> Detector : row exists (entity has a reference route)

note over Detector
  If no row found: entity has no reference route.
  Skip without publishing to deviation.candidates.
  Real ADS-B/AIS entities fall through here in v1.
end note

Detector -> RoutePoints : SELECT sequence_index, lat, lon, tolerance_metres\nFROM route_reference_points\nWHERE route_id = $route_id\nORDER BY sequence_index
RoutePoints --> Detector : ordered waypoint list

@enduml
